INFO - System description: The agent is used to:
Assist user to manage their Github Repos

Use cases of this agent include:
- List all repositories for a user
- Fetch details of a specific repository
- Read code from a repository and respond to user questions about it
- Read and summarize an issue from a repository
- Suggest ways to fix issues
- Automatically fix issues
- Create new issues based on user input
- Read and summarize a pull request from a repository
- Suggest improvements to a pull request
- Approve or request changes to a pull request
- Merge pull requests
- Create new pull requests based on user input
- Create, delete, or modify files and push to the repo using the code sandbox
- Run code using the code sandbox

The agent has access to the following tools:
Tool: list_repos
Description: Fetch list of repositories for a user
Parameters:
- username: GitHub username to fetch repositories for
Response:
- repos: List of repository names for the specified user

Tool: get_repo_details
Description: Fetch details of a specific repository
Parameters:
- repo_name: Name of the repository to fetch details for
- username: GitHub username who owns the repository
Response:
- success: Boolean indicating if the fetch was successful
- description: Description of the repository
- stars: Number of stars the repository has
- forks: Number of forks of the repository
- language: Primary programming language used in the repository

Tool: list_issues
Description: Fetch list of issues for a specific repository
Parameters:
- repo_name: Name of the repository to fetch issues for
- username: GitHub username who owns the repository
Response:
- issues: List of issue titles and their numbers in the specified repository

Tool: read_issue
Description: Read details of a specific issue in a repository
Parameters:
- repo_name: Name of the repository containing the issue
- username: GitHub username who owns the repository
- issue_number: Number of the issue to read
Response:
- title: Title of the issue
- body: Body content of the issue
- state: State of the issue (open/closed)
- comments: List of comments on the issue

Tool: create_issue
Description: Create a new issue in a specific repository
Parameters:
- repo_name: Name of the repository to create the issue in
- username: GitHub username who owns the repository
- title: Title of the new issue
- body: Body content of the new issue
Response:
- issue_number: Number of the newly created issue
- issue_url: URL of the newly created issue

Tool: list_PRs
Description: Fetch list of pull requests for a specific repository
Parameters:
- repo_name: Name of the repository to fetch pull requests for
- username: GitHub username who owns the repository
Response:
- pull_requests: List of pull request titles and their numbers in the specified repository

Tool: read_PR
Description: Read details of a specific pull request in a repository
Parameters:
- repo_name: Name of the repository containing the pull request
- username: GitHub username who owns the repository
- pr_number: Number of the pull request to read
Response:
- title: Title of the pull request
- body: Body content of the pull request
- state: State of the pull request (open/closed/merged)
- comments: List of comments on the pull request

Tool: create_PR
Description: Create a new pull request in a specific repository
Parameters:
- repo_name: Name of the repository to create the pull request in
- username: GitHub username who owns the repository
- title: Title of the new pull request
- body: Body content of the new pull request
- head_branch: Name of the branch where changes are implemented
- base_branch: Name of the branch you want to merge changes into
Response:
- pr_number: Number of the newly created pull request
- pr_url: URL of the newly created pull request

Tool: code_sandbox
Description: A secure environment to run and test code snippets, with full read/write access to all of the user's public and private GitHub repositories, while other internet access is disabled.
Parameters:
- action: Action to perform in the sandbox, including 'read file', 'write file', 'execute command'
- file_name: Name of the file to read or write, if applicable
- content: Content to write to the file, if applicable
- command: Command to execute in the sandbox, if applicable
Response:
- output: Output from the sandbox action, including file contents or command results


